T024 evidence, second capture: User Story 1 on hardware, 2026-10-28, image built from
the clean commit 2ac5f5c (/etc/build-info says VERSION=2ac5f5c, no -dirty).

Excerpts only; the full boot logs are long and repetitive.

--- the seed fix works: the empty slot is no longer reported good -----------------

# rauc status
=== System Info ===
Compatible:  ota-beaglebone-black
Booted from: rootfs.0 (/dev/mmcblk0p2)
=== Bootloader ===
Activated: rootfs.0 (A)
=== Slot States ===
o [rootfs.1] (/dev/mmcblk0p3, ext4, inactive)
      bootname: B
      boot status: bad
x [rootfs.0] (/dev/mmcblk0p2, ext4, booted)
      bootname: A
      boot status: good

# fw_printenv BOOT_ORDER BOOT_A_LEFT BOOT_B_LEFT   (after one boot)
BOOT_ORDER=A
BOOT_A_LEFT=2
BOOT_B_LEFT=0

--- the stored values are complete ------------------------------------------------

The two lines that printenv and fw_printenv both showed cut off at exactly 238
characters (name included) are not cut off on the card:

# fw_printenv -n ota_select | wc -c
240
# fw_printenv -n ota_load_boot | wc -c
238

Those are the full lengths (239 and 237 characters plus a newline). The cut-off is in the
capture path, not in U-Boot, Linux or the stored environment. This matters because the
tail of ota_select is the last-resort path.

--- the write direction, Linux to U-Boot -----------------------------------------

# rauc status mark-good
rauc-event-Message: Marked slot rootfs.0 as good
# fw_printenv BOOT_A_LEFT
BOOT_A_LEFT=3

Power was then removed while Linux was running. The first attempt to come back up was
interrupted again during the U-Boot countdown (no attempt spent), and the next one
booted:

Hit any key to stop autoboot: 0
Saving Environment to MMC... Writing to redundant MMC(0)... OK
OTA: booting slot A, 2 attempts left

3 minus the one attempt just spent. Had the write from Linux landed anywhere other than
the offsets U-Boot reads, U-Boot would have seen 2 and announced 1. This closes the gap
left open in t016-env-agreement.log.

--- /data survives an unclean power loss ------------------------------------------

[    3.122038] EXT4-fs (mmcblk0p4): recovery complete
[    3.129215] EXT4-fs (mmcblk0p4): mounted filesystem ... r/w with ordered data mode.
# ls -l /data/keep
-rw-r--r--    1 root     root             0 Jan  1 00:02 /data/keep

The file was written and synced, then the power was cut with Linux running. The journal
was replayed on the next mount and the file is there.

--- the running slot is read-only --------------------------------------------------

[    2.789350] EXT4-fs (mmcblk0p2): mounted filesystem ... ro with ordered data mode.
[    2.801755] VFS: Mounted root (ext4 filesystem) readonly on device 179:2.

and no "re-mounted ... r/w" line afterwards. The next finding is itself proof: a write
into the root filesystem failed with "Read-only file system".

--- finding: seedrng cannot write its seed -----------------------------------------

seedrng: can't create directory '/var/lib/seedrng': Read-only file system

Every boot. Buildroot's skeleton points most of /var at tmpfs, but /var/lib is a real
directory in the rootfs, and seedrng writes its seed there. An earlier commit message
said nothing writes to /var/lib at boot; that was wrong. The init script documents the
fix itself (SEEDRNG_ARGS="--seed-dir=/data/seedrng" in /etc/default/seedrng), and
post-build.sh now writes that file for A/B boards. To be confirmed on the next flash.

--- not shown by this capture -------------------------------------------------------

Boot time (T025 needs grabserial). The seedrng fix on hardware. The watchdog, which is
unchanged: omap_wdt registers with a 60 s timeout and nothing resets the board (T032).
